          "param": "m"
        }
      ],
      "why": "pipelined_m registers every m stages, so it still yields 1 result/cycle (throughput = Fmax) but with fewer pipeline registers than fully pipelined, lowering FF count and power_index at the cost of a longer combinational path per stage (m micro-rotations in series). m=2..4 keeps the per-stage path short enough to plausibly hold Fmax >= 50 MHz while cutting register area/power versus the m=1 case. Same data_width/n_iter/guard/rounding ranges as pipelined so the accuracy axis is comparable and the fronts merge cleanly. This family should expose the low-power corner of the front (fewer FFs, lower power_index) at slightly reduced Fmax margin, which is exactly the trade-off the spec wants mapped."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The spec needs throughput >= 50 MSPS, which for a 1-result/cycle architecture means Fmax >= 50 MHz, plus max_abs_err <= 2^-12. Both pipelined and pipelined_m produce one result per cycle, so they are the natural fit (iterative/unrolled_k would need Fmax >= 50*N and are not in my assigned set anyway). I split the 100-eval budget 50/50 between the two families. pipelined (m=1) is the safe, high-Fmax baseline that reliably clears the throughput constraint; pipelined_m (m=2..4) trades a longer per-stage path for fewer registers, targeting the low-power_index end of the front. I keep n_iter in 12..20 because N>=12 is the minimum to reach the 2^-12 accuracy floor and N>20 adds area/power with diminishing accuracy. data_width 14..18 gives 12..16 fractional output bits, enough to satisfy the error bound with small guard bits. angle_guard -2..2 and frac_guard 0..2 span the accuracy-vs-area knobs that shape the accuracy_bits objective. Rounding is fixed to 'round' since it only helps accuracy at negligible cost. This covers the full power-vs-accuracy trade-off curve so the hypervolume is maximized and the min-power feasible point is found."
}

{
  "decision": "stop",
  "rationale": "The feasible Pareto front is well-established with 7 distinct designs covering the trade-off space effectively. The minimum power_index achieved is 2.1 (pipelined_m, data_width=16, n_iter=14), which satisfies the throughput constraint (78.8 MSPS >= 50) and accuracy constraint (12.22 bits >= 12). The front extends to higher accuracy (14.7 bits) at the cost of power (4.93). The hypervolume of 99.03 indicates good coverage relative to the reference point (40, 12). Since this is the final round and the front clearly identifies the optimal design for the selection rule (min power_index) as the pipelined_m design with power_index=2.1, further exploration is unnecessary. The constraints are met, and the trade-off curve is mapped sufficiently to select the best design."
,
